{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Mei, sorry Excel is getting in your way. Your account looks fine (it's active and not locked). The \"Unlicensed Product\" message usually means Office lost track of who's signed in, and signing in again normally fixes it. Please try these steps: 1) In Excel, go to File > Account and click Sign out. 2) Close all Office apps (Excel, Word, Outlook, Teams). 3) Restart your laptop. 4) Open Excel and sign in with your company email (mei.chen@brightline.example), not a personal Microsoft account. 5) If it still shows the message, go to Windows Settings > Accounts > Access work or school and remove any old or duplicate accounts. Then sign in to Excel again. If you still see \"Unlicensed Product\" after signing in with your company email, reply to this ticket and we'll pass it to our licensing team to check your license. One more thing: your mailbox is about 99% full. Please delete or archive some old email soon so you don't stop receiving messages.",
  "internal_note": "KB-026 followed. lookup_user (user_id 9): account active, 0 failed logins, not blocked, must_change_password false, password last set 2026-09-11. Device BL-LT-0109. Nothing on the account explains the problem. Sent the user the KB-026 steps: sign out and back in with the company account, restart, remove duplicate work/school accounts. I have no tool to check license assignment. If the user signs in correctly and still sees Unlicensed Product, escalate to Tier 2 to check the M365 license (KB-026 escalation criterion). Separate issue: mailbox is at 99.4% of its 50 GB quota. Told the user to clean it up.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-026"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
